  [1mSection 1: Components and interface[0m

  [1mNew file [38;5;153msession.js[39m.[0m This is the only file that touches storage.

  [34mconst[39m SESSION_KEY = [31m"session"[39m;

  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33msaveSession[39m({ userId, username })  [32m// writes JSON to localStorage[39m
  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mgetCurrentUser[39m()                   [32m// returns { userId, username, loggedInAt } or null[39m
  [34mexport[39m [34mfunction[39m [33mclearSession[39m()                     [32m// removes the key[39m

  - The stored value is [38;5;153m{ userId, username, loggedInAt }[39m as JSON, under one key. [38;5;153mloggedInAt[39m is an ISO timestamp, which
    covers the "track who logged in" goal cheaply.
  - If the stored value is missing or corrupt (bad JSON, no [38;5;153muserId[39m), [38;5;153mgetCurrentUser()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m instead of throwing.
    Other forms can then treat that case as "not logged in".

  [1mChanges to [38;5;153mapp.js[39m:[0m
  - [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m keeps its current inputs. [1mNo [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter.[0m It returns [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m. Until
    there's a real API, the stub derives [38;5;153muserId[39m from the username (for example [38;5;153m"user-" + username[39m), and a comment marks
    it as a placeholder for the server's ID.
  - If login succeeds, the submit handler calls [38;5;153msaveSession(...)[39m. On failure it saves nothing.
  - A [38;5;153mlogout()[39m in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m calls [38;5;153mclearSession()[39m. It's exported for future use, and I won't add a logout button unless you
    want one.

  [1mChange to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m:[0m [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js"></script>[39m.

  [1mLeaving [38;5;153msrc/[39m alone.[0m That's a separate Node entry point ([38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m, [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m) and has nothing to do with the
  browser app.

